---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="Account addresses">
  <h1>Account addresses</h1>
  <p>
    A project can issue every account an <strong>account address</strong> on a
    domain the project owns. People keep signing up with whatever they have, an
    email at any provider or a username, and each account also gets an address
    on your domain. A consumer product gets a stable, project-unique handle for
    every account. A workplace gets addresses its own mail system can deliver
    to.
  </p>

  <h2>Turning it on</h2>
  <p>
    Set <code>accounts.domain</code> in the project's config. The default
    project takes it from <code>GATEWAY_DEFAULT_EMAIL_DOMAIN</code>. Leaving it
    empty, the default, issues no addresses.
  </p>
  <Code lang="json" code={`{
  "accounts": {
    "domain": "accounts.example.com"
  }
}`} />
  <p>
    The domain is stored lower-cased, without a trailing dot and IDN-punycoded,
    and must be a fully qualified name. A domain that fails these checks fails
    the config write. Mail reaches the addresses only if the domain's MX records
    point at a mail system that delivers them. Issuing an address does not set
    up mail.
  </p>

  <h2>How an address is formed</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Username accounts</strong> get <code>&lt;username&gt;@&lt;domain&gt;</code>:
      <code>bob</code> becomes <code>bob@accounts.example.com</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Email accounts</strong> keep their whole email, with the
      <code>@</code> written as <code>-at-</code>:
      <code>bob@mail.example</code> becomes
      <code>bob-at-mail.example@accounts.example.com</code>. Two people named Bob
      at different providers get different addresses, and the plain
      <code>bob@accounts.example.com</code> stays free for a username account.
    </li>
    <li>
      Letters are lower-cased. Every character other than <code>a–z</code>,
      <code>0–9</code>, <code>.</code>, <code>_</code>, <code>+</code> and
      <code>-</code> becomes <code>-</code>, and runs of dots collapse, so the
      address is always valid.
    </li>
    <li>
      If the part before the <code>@</code> is longer than 64 characters (the
      limit for an email address), it keeps its first characters and ends in
      eight hex digits of a hash of the whole value. The same account always
      gets the same address.
    </li>
    <li>
      If the address is already taken, the account gets the next free suffix:
      <code>-2</code>, <code>-3</code> and so on. After twenty, a suffix
      derived from the account's id ends the search.
    </li>
    <li>
      New usernames may not contain <code>-at-</code>, so no new username can
      take the address another person's email derives to. Usernames created
      before this rule keep working as they are.
    </li>
    <li>Anonymous accounts get no address until they are upgraded.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When it is issued</h2>
  <p>
    A username account gets its address when it is created, by self-signup,
    by an admin or by a guardian. An email account gets its address once its
    email is verified: at sign-up when the sign-up proves the address (OAuth,
    passkey, an emailed code), otherwise at the first sign-in after
    verification. An address spells the email, so it is never issued for an
    email nobody has proven they own. An account that existed before the
    project set a domain gets its address at its next sign-in or session
    refresh, and so do accounts provisioned by SCIM.
  </p>
  <p>
    An address is written only to an account that has none, so it is never
    replaced behind the account's back, and changing the project's domain
    leaves issued addresses as they were. The one exception is an address
    derived from an email: when the person confirms a change of email, the
    address follows it, so it never goes on spelling out an address they gave
    up. A username's address is the account's own handle and stays. A project
    that stops issuing addresses keeps the ones it issued. An address released
    by an email change can later be issued to another account, so treat the
    address as a handle and key on the user id. Failing to
    store an address never blocks a sign-in: the server logs it and tries again
    at the next sign-in.
  </p>

  <h2>Where it appears</h2>
  <p>
    <code>User.account_address</code> carries it, on <code>GetCurrentUser</code>
    and on the admin user reads.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
